# THE COLONIZATION OF THE BODY BY MODERN MEDICINE
## Field Notes from Inside the American Healthcare System

*For A Somatic Unraveling — socioeconomic layer / structural erasure chapter*

---

## THE ENCOUNTER

First annual physical with a traditional PCP after years of using a direct primary care provider — DPC dropped when funds ran out. A high-tier ACA marketplace plan. The encounter was preceded by a form requiring a signature agreeing not to discuss the chronic condition — the cervical spine condition that is literally the dominant structural parameter of daily life. The core health issue that governs every hour was contractually excluded from the room where the body was supposedly being cared for.

The doctor is kind. That matters, and it makes it worse.

## WHAT HAPPENED IN THE ROOM

Blood pressure check — elevated. Blood work. Stethoscope. That was it.

The entire clinical encounter amounted to the checkbox version of a body. The doctor listened to brief references to actual needs — a hormonal intervention to reduce inflammation impacting the neck condition — and nodded. Just nodded. Because there was nowhere for that information to go inside the system she's operating in. She heard it, received it, understood it, and could do nothing with it.

Pain management prescriptions — the medications that directly determine daily function — were off the table. Everything that would have constituted actual care was off the table.

The high blood pressure wasn't even noted in the chart. The visible exhaustion — bags under the eyes, diminished vitality, a body ground down by weeks of PT — wasn't engaged. The doctor couldn't give a shit. Not because she doesn't care. Because the container won't let her.

## THE FORM IS THE THING

Before the body even sat down, the system required signing away the right to be a whole person in the room. That is not bureaucracy. That is the colonial move — a terms-of-service agreement for access to your own health. The system will serve you, but only the version of you it can process. Leave the rest at the door.

And the thing that makes it colonial specifically: the patient walked in with more data and more intelligence about her own condition than the system was willing to receive. A sophisticated somatic tracking system. Deep into a PT protocol with a provider who actually engages the full picture. Years of embodied practice. Clinical-grade self-awareness. The knowledge exists. The body is speaking. The system structurally cannot hear it and has built paperwork to formalize the not-hearing.

## PARTICIPATING IN YOUR OWN ERASURE

The phrase that names it. Knowing why you're doing it — strategically, for the referral — doesn't make the body experience of doing it hurt less. You still have to sit there and be less than you are. The depression afterward is the cost of that participation. The body refused to metabolize the experience all day because the experience is indigestible. You can't compost something that's still actively happening to you in real time.

The gap between intellectual knowledge and somatic knowledge cracked open: you knew this was how the system worked for other people. Having your own depleted body sit in that chair and get nothing is a completely different kind of knowledge. The body remembers what care felt like when you could pay for it — a DPC who saw you, lined things up, held the full picture. Now the body is in the same condition or worse, and the system handed it a form that said *be less.*

## THE CASCADE — INSURER, CODING, AND THE SURGEON

The structural failures stack. A previous PT coded visits incorrectly. The insurer charged for weekly "surgeries" for months. The insurer admitted the error. The clinic still hasn't discharged the false charges because the insurer didn't refile the claims. The clinic staff said to ignore the billing notices — they'll discharge at year-end. But the unresolved billing eliminates access to the surgeon.

The surgeon who already dismissed the patient earlier in the year because she *looked healthy.* The years of somatic practice, the capacity to hold yourself upright, the refusal to perform collapse — all of it works against you in a system that runs visual triage. The skills that keep you functional read as evidence that you don't need care. Meanwhile, the waiting room full of seniors on public insurance in obvious agony aren't getting better care — they just passed the visual threshold. The system doesn't help them either. It just sees them first because their suffering is legible in the language the system recognizes.

Caught between two erasures: too functional to be seen by the surgeon, too complex to be heard by the PCP. And a billing error nobody will fix is the bureaucratic lock that keeps the whole thing sealed.

## THE LIFE COACH

The pain clinic the PCP originally tried to refer to wanted to pair the patient with a life coach. For a complex cervical spine condition. As if the problem is attitude toward pain rather than the pain itself. When the patient refused — the only sane response — the clinic refused to reassign. The system said *this is what we have for you* and when the patient said *that's not care* the system said *then you get nothing.*

The pipeline has one destination regardless of what the body actually needs.

## THE REFERRAL — WHAT WAS ACTUALLY EXTRACTED

The real pain clinic was found in the back of a book written by a doctor who runs a university pain clinic. Not through the system's channels — those lead to life coaches. Found through research intelligence, the documentary eye following the trail of practitioners doing real work, mapping backward to who trained them and where they practice.

Access to adequate care required: the ability to read specialist literature, the clinical vocabulary to evaluate what was found, the self-advocacy to refuse what was offered, and a referral from a PCP whose only real function was to sign the paper that opened the door. The entire system between the patient and care is either obstacle or rubber stamp. The actual navigation happened in the patient's own intelligence.

The referral was the extraction. Walked into a system that couldn't hold you, signed the form that erased you, sat through the nod and the cuff and the nothing — and got the one thing you went in for. Strategic operation inside a hostile architecture.

## THE FINANCIAL FRAME TIGHTENING

The marketplace insurer is pulling out. Months of coverage remaining. No participation in the marketplace next year — a sovereignty act, not a consumer choice. Refusing the terms-of-service agreement for access to your own body.

Future care: full-priced health plans plus out-of-pocket DPC. Most of the care that actually works already lives outside insurance coverage. The care insurance will cover — PT, which is genuinely working — requires correct coding, meaning healing depends on an administrative translation layer between what's actually happening in the body and what the system recognizes as reimbursable.

The body doesn't heal on a policy calendar. The PT protocol has its own timeline — autonomic headroom, delayed-onset cycles, bilateral tracking. None of that cares when the insurer leaves the marketplace. But both clocks run simultaneously — the body's clock and the coverage clock — and they have nothing to do with each other.

## THE SOCIOECONOMIC CHAPTER WROTE ITSELF

Couldn't have been written from analysis. Arrived in the body, as the documentary photographer's stories always do. The triptych form holds it exactly:

- **Documentary image** — the clinical encounter as it happened
- **Original field notes** — what was said and not said in that room
- **Present-tense body notes** — what it felt like to participate in your own erasure while visibly depleted and be handed nothing

The demoralizing truth underneath all of it: *being taken seriously* is what you're hoping for from a specialist referral. That's not a high bar. That's the floor. And the system has made the floor feel like a gift.

An almost inaccessible gift.

## WHAT THIS CHAPTER CARRIES FOR THE BOOK

This is where the memoir's scope cracks open beyond the individual body. The socioeconomic layer isn't context or backdrop — it's the architecture that determines what healing is available, to whom, and under what conditions. The body's healing timeline runs inside a financial and bureaucratic architecture that has its own logic, its own timeline, and its own terms of engagement — none of which have anything to do with the body.

The chapter proves it from the inside: a patient with clinical vocabulary, somatic literacy, research intelligence, self-advocacy, and a high-tier marketplace plan walked into a doctor's office and got a blood pressure cuff and a nod. If that's the experience with every advantage except money — what is everyone else getting?

The answer: the nod. The life coach. The billing error no one will fix. The form that says *be less.*

